Tracking
========

A desktop library for your anime, movies, TV shows and games, spread over any
number of folders and drives. It scans them, sorts everything into Anime / Movies /
TV Shows / Games
(games are split by platform: PC, PS1-PS5, Saturn, Dreamcast, Switch, ...), and
shows a poster wall with ratings and total time:

  * Anime  - AniDB, MyAnimeList, AniList, Kitsu, Trakt; total runtime of all episodes
  * Movies - IMDb, Rotten Tomatoes, Metacritic, TMDB, Trakt; total runtime
  * TV     - IMDb, TMDB, TVmaze, Trakt; total runtime of all episodes, one item per season
  * Games  - Metacritic, OpenCritic, IGDB, Steam reviews, RAWG, HLTB users;
             HowLongToBeat main / main+extras / completionist hours

Each card shows every site's score as small chips plus a "★" average (all scores
scaled to 0-10); sort by "Score: average" or "Score: highest".

Click any item to open its page: mark it as watched / beaten, see every score,
the episode list with per-episode scores (TV: TVmaze, or TMDB with a key; anime:
MyAnimeList, or AniDB with a client) and which episodes are on disk, read the
summary, edit the title / category / platform / year, and keep notes.


Running
-------

    run.bat        starts dist\Tracking\Tracking.exe (builds it first if it's missing)
    build.bat      builds a fresh Tracking.exe and runs its self-test

The build (build.ps1 + packaging\Tracking.spec, using PyInstaller) creates the
virtual environment on first use, bundles Python, Qt, MediaInfo and the parsing
libraries into dist\Tracking\ (~140 MB), and then runs `Tracking.exe --selftest`,
which checks guessit, MediaInfo, Qt and the rest work inside the exe. Zip the
dist\Tracking folder to move it to another PC; no Python needed there. Rebuild
after changing the code.

From source, without building:

    python -m venv .venv
    .venv\Scripts\pip install -r requirements.txt
    .venv\Scripts\python -m tracking

On first start the Locations window opens. Add a whole drive or a folder, pick
what it contains (or leave "Auto-detect"), and it is scanned right away.

No key needed (always on):

  * Anime: MyAnimeList (Jikan), AniList, Kitsu
  * Games: HowLongToBeat (times, covers), Steam reviews + Metacritic for PC games
  * TV: TVmaze (rating, poster, summary), IMDb posters and links
  * Movies: IMDb posters and links (no rating without a key)

Free keys, entered under Settings (one tab per category):

  * OMDb key             IMDb / Rotten Tomatoes / Metacritic   https://www.omdbapi.com/apikey.aspx
  * TMDB key or token    TMDB score, better posters            https://www.themoviedb.org/settings/api
  * Trakt Client ID      Trakt score (movies + anime)          https://trakt.tv/oauth/applications/new
  * AniDB client + ver.  AniDB score                           https://anidb.net/software/add
  * RAWG key             Metacritic, description              https://rawg.io/apidocs
  * IGDB (Twitch) ID +   IGDB critic/user scores, covers,      https://dev.twitch.tv/console/apps
    secret               great for retro consoles
  * OpenCritic key       OpenCritic score (via RapidAPI)       https://rapidapi.com (OpenCritic API)


Where things are stored
-----------------------

Everything lives in %LOCALAPPDATA%\Tracking (override with TRACKING_DATA_DIR):

    library.sqlite3   all items, scores, completion state, notes, locations
    posters\          downloaded cover art / thumbnails, one file per item
    cache\            cached API responses (keeps us within rate limits)

A cover.jpg / folder.jpg / poster.jpg inside an item's folder is used instead
of downloading one.

Export / import: Settings > General > "Export..." writes one .zip, either the
database only (tiny) or database + cover images. API keys are left out unless you
tick the box, so an export is safe to share. "Import..." either replaces your
library with an export or adds its items to yours (your edits and notes win; an
item counts as completed if either copy is; your API keys are kept).

Portable mode: create a folder named "data" next to Tracking.exe and the app
keeps its library there instead, so the exe and its data travel together
(USB stick, synced folder). Drives are still recognised on another PC by their
filesystem serial.


Drives are tracked by id, not by letter
---------------------------------------

Each location is stored as (volume id, path inside the volume):

  * local disks: the Windows volume GUID, plus the filesystem serial number so the
    same disk is recognised on another PC; disks without a drive letter work too
  * mapped network drives: the UNC share (\\nas\media)
  * Linux: the filesystem UUID

So a USB disk that comes back as F: instead of E: keeps all its items.


Items are not tied to a drive
-----------------------------

  * Unplugged drive: its items stay, shown dimmed as "drive offline".
  * Removed location: you choose to keep its items (default) or remove them.
  * Moved files: when an item that disappeared shows up on another drive or
    folder, the existing entry is re-linked, keeping its completed state and notes.
  * Rescan (F5) lets you pick locations and has an option
    "Mark items that are no longer found as completed", for when you delete
    things after finishing them. Missing items are never deleted.

Filters: "Hide completed" and "Hide missing" next to the search box.

Metadata is fetched for several items at once (Settings > General, default 4;
1 = one by one). Each site's own rate limit still applies, so this overlaps
waiting on different sites rather than hitting any one harder. Games are bounded
by Steam's limit (about 3 s per PC game); 24 games took 61 s instead of 115 s.

Jobs never block each other: starting a scan or metadata update while another
runs queues it (shown as "+N queued" in the status bar); scans go to the front
of the queue. Cancel stops only the running job; finished work is kept.


How folders are recognised
--------------------------

  * Folders named Anime / Movies / Films / Games / ROMs are containers.
  * Platform folders are containers too: "PS2", "PC Games", "Saturn ISOs",
    "Sony - PlayStation 3", "Nintendo 64", ...
  * Anything else inside a container is one item (a series, a movie, a game).
  * Loose files are grouped by title, so "Game (Disc 1).cue" + "(Disc 2)" or
    "[Group] Show - 01..24.mkv" become one item.
  * With no folder hints, contents decide: ROM extensions, disc headers
    (Saturn, Dreamcast, PS1/PS2/PSP/PS3, GameCube, Wii), PS3_GAME folders, .exe
    for PC, and fansub-style names for anime.

  * Platform for ambiguous files (.iso, .bin, .chd, .pkg, .zip/.7z/.rar), in order:
    platform folder > ROM extension > release tags in the name ("[FitGirl PC Repack]"
    -> PC, a Switch title id like [01004E90149AA000] -> Switch, "(PS2)") > .exe
    present (PC) > disc header > platform words anywhere in the path ("ps2 isos").
    Archives with no platform hint at all are skipped.
  * Video with episode numbers but no anime markers (Bad.Sisters.S01...) goes to
    TV Shows, as do folders named TV / TV Shows / Series / Shows. Seasons and
    scattered episodes are grouped the same way as anime (below).
  * "Unknown" holds whatever you move there yourself.
  * A folder with any name ("tpex") holding 3+ releases is treated as a container.
  * Disc / store serials in names give the platform (BCES/BLUS/NPEB -> PS3,
    ULUS -> PSP, CUSA -> PS4); Redump region tags "(USA)" and GOG-Games names
    ("game-title-(12345)") mark archives as games.
  * Updates, patches and DLC ("X.Update.v1.2-RUNE", Switch ids ending in 800,
    "DLC Pack" folders) are counted under their game, not listed on their own.
  * Two copies of the same game or movie are one item (a movie's runtime counts once).
  * ROMs of a different game dropped inside a game folder become their own item.
  * Skipped: macOS "._" files, scrambled names, firmware / key dumps / emulators.

Anime grouping:

  * One item per series + season: a folder with "Season 1" / "Season 2" (or
    S01Exx / S02Exx files) becomes two items; specials join season 1.
  * Episodes of one show spread over a folder and loose files are merged, so the
    episode count and total runtime cover all of them.
  * Anime movies (no episode number) are separate items, never merged into a series.
  * Extras folders (NCOP/NCED, menus, trailers, ...) don't count as episodes.

Anything it gets wrong can be fixed on the item page or with right-click > Move to.
Select several cards (Ctrl/Shift+click, Ctrl+A) to move, complete or refetch them
in bulk from the bar above the grid. Each item page shows why it was detected the
way it was.


Logs
----

Logs go to %LOCALAPPDATA%\Tracking\logs\tracking.log (rotated, 5 x 2 MB). Pick the
level in Settings > General: DEBUG explains every classification decision and
every service match (use it when something is misdetected), INFO gives scan and
fetch summaries. The Log button shows recent lines with level and text filters.


Code layout and how to add a service, section or platform: docs/ARCHITECTURE.md

Tests
-----

    .venv\Scripts\pip install pytest
    .venv\Scripts\python -m pytest
